questions: 1000|c0cca7ab-6e65-5835-b308-4b22c9ebfef2|4|<h2>Design a new Elicit survey</h2><p>This tool interviews you about the shape of the survey you want to build — its steps (pages) and the sections on each one. Every answer maps onto one row of the <code>.elicit</code> definition file that gets imported into an Elicit deployment.</p><p><b>This interview sets up the structure only.</b> What you produce here is a skeleton: the survey's identity, its steps, the sections on each step, and how results are delivered. Everything that goes inside that skeleton is added afterwards in the <b>Author</b> tool:</p><ul><li>the <b>questions</b> in each section, with their wording, answer type, validation and help text;</li><li>the <b>answer lists</b> (select groups and items) those questions draw on;</li><li>the <b>rules</b> — which question is watched, the test applied to its answer (equals, is greater than, is checked, has been answered, contains) and the <b>action</b> taken: show, repeat, or replace text;</li><li><b>tokens</b> in wording so text changes with earlier answers;</li><li>the reporting <b>tags</b> (ontology and metadata) that shape exports and reports;</li><li><b>preview and testing</b> of the logic before anything is published.</li></ul><p>You can stop and resume; nothing is published until an author reviews your answers.</p>|intro||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1001|7a575b1e-07f1-54af-9850-82f93af0391d|4|<h3>How an Elicit survey is put together</h3><p>Every survey is a small hierarchy. Knowing the names helps, because the rest of this interview and the author who builds the survey both use them.</p><dl><dt><b>Survey</b></dt><dd>The top-level record with name, title, description, and finish URL (Family History Survey, Library Card Registration, etc.).</dd><dt><b>Steps</b></dt><dd>The pages in the progress bar; a step can repeat per person (Welcome, Demographics, etc.).</dd><dt><b>Sections</b></dt><dd>Headed groups on a page; one section can be mounted under several steps and share its questions (Your Mother, Cancers, etc.).</dd><dt><b>Questions</b></dt><dd>The answer kinds available and the fields each carries ("What is your current age?", "Are you the patient?", etc.).</dd><dt><b>Select groups and select items</b></dt><dd>Reusable answer lists with display text and a stored coded value (Yes/No, Male/Female, etc.).</dd><dt><b>Relationships</b></dt><dd>The upstream question, operator, and show/repeat/replace-text action (show the current-age question when a relative is Alive, repeat the child's-name question once per child, etc.). Replace-text uses tokens: "What is &#123;S1's&#124;your&#125; age?" reads "What is Anna's age?" when someone is answering for Anna.</dd><dt><b>Reports and post-survey actions</b></dt><dd>What happens to a finished response (Family Pedigree, Cancer Summary, etc.).</dd><dt><b>Ontology, dimensions and metadata</b></dt><dd>Tags that shape the reporting layer (Age, Breast Cancer, etc.).</dd></dl><p>This interview asks only for the <b>survey</b>, its <b>steps</b> and <b>sections</b>, and how results are delivered. An author adds the questions, answer lists, relationships and reporting tags afterwards, using your outline.</p>|anatomy||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1002|c43474c4-0537-5bb6-b189-23fb74a7d970|4|<h3>Survey identity</h3><p><b>What this captures.</b> The single <code>surveys</code> record that everything else hangs off: the machine name, the title respondents see, a description of its purpose, and where respondents go when they finish.</p><p><b>How it is used.</b> The machine name is how administrators find the survey when they generate access codes and pull exports, so it must be unique in the deployment. The title appears at the top of every page respondents see. The description is shown to administrators, not respondents. The owner's email is for the author to reach you with questions; it is not stored in the survey.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> The name and title are the hardest things to change once respondents have been invited, because access codes, emails and exports all refer to them. Pick them as if they were permanent.</p>|identity_intro||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1003|d07e6653-1dff-5d69-b864-7181de7dc847|8|Short machine name for the survey (letters and digits, no spaces)|SurveyName|Becomes surveys.name. Must be unique within the deployment.|true|||Use letters and digits only, no spaces.|||LibraryCardReg|||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
