questions: 1007|6f0d3411-fd80-5230-9b7d-9b6ad36d2092|8|Where should respondents be sent after they finish? (leave blank for the default thank-you page)|PostSurveyUrl||false||||||https://example.org/thank-you|||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1008|c4ee1b8f-804a-5993-8fb2-a6d72cf22486|4|<h3>Steps and sections</h3><p><b>What this captures.</b> The skeleton of the survey. A <b>step</b> is one page the respondent moves through; the progress bar lists the steps by name. A <b>section</b> is a headed group of questions on a step. Each step you describe becomes a <code>steps</code> record, each heading a <code>sections</code> record, and each pairing a <code>steps_sections</code> record.</p><p><b>How it is used.</b> Every question the author adds later is placed in one of these sections, so the outline you give here decides where everything lives. The short <i>report names</i> become the dimension names the reporting tables and pedigree drawings are built from — keep them short, plain and stable. A section can be re-mounted under several steps; it then shares its questions between them, which is how one "Cancers" block can serve a mother, a father and every sibling without being written eight times.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> Step and section positions are baked into every stored answer's key. Adding a step later is easy; reordering or removing one after respondents have started is not. Get the order right here, even if the wording changes.</p>|structure_intro||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1009|db2b2c8e-633f-5d6e-aeed-8747ee9e271c|5|How many steps (pages) should the survey have?|StepCount|Each step is one page the respondent moves through.|true|1|20|Enter a whole number between 1 and 20.||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1010|8400a055-a4ab-5a87-bfcd-1e722d129f5f|8|Name of this step, as shown in the progress bar|StepName||true|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1011|322491cc-458e-5d58-9297-a333fbaf8db5|8|Short name used for this step in reports and data exports|StepReportName|Becomes steps.dimension_name. Leave blank to reuse the step name.|false||||||Mother|||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1012|98056e73-7bbd-5a69-aea4-704f64cf3396|9|The sections on this step, one per line and in order, written as Heading=ReportName|SectionHeadings|Example: Your Mother=Mother on one line. Leave out =ReportName to reuse the heading. A step needs at least one section.|true|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1013|3ce7b0c3-d273-5ec1-8844-d21b8dabac5a|9|If any of these sections already appear on an earlier step, list their headings here, one per line|ReusedSections|The same section can sit under several steps and shares its questions between them. Write the heading exactly as it appeared on the earlier step.|false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1014|c1b2a2c9-f995-5d88-9925-374b9e42f956|4|<h3>Reports and follow-up</h3><p><b>What this captures.</b> What happens to a completed response. Each report you name becomes a <code>reports</code> record; a webhook becomes a <code>post_survey_actions</code> record.</p><p><b>How it is used.</b> Reports appear as buttons on the respondent's final page and in the Admin app, and are rendered on demand by the service at the URL you give. Post-survey actions run automatically the moment a respondent finishes — for example, generating a PDF and dropping it on an SFTP server for the clinic. Email notification and "nothing" need no further detail.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> This is the only part of the survey that reaches people other than the respondent. If you are unsure, choose "Nothing": data stays in Elicit and can be exported by an administrator later, whereas a wrong endpoint fails silently at completion time.</p>|delivery_intro||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1015|f649e0f8-8876-5213-94ed-edd349368179|11|How should completed responses be delivered?|DeliveryChannels||false||||1|||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1016|650e322f-c5b0-53cc-8aa0-807d22b671c8|8|What should the report be called?|ReportName||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1017|32a986a0-177a-5968-9352-54b1faf79334|8|Report or webhook endpoint URL|ReportUrl||false||||||http://report-service:8080/report|||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
questions: 1018|e0abcd20-816b-53b1-83f6-6ebea384c453|4|<h3>Review and hand-off</h3><p><b>What this captures.</b> Your confirmation that the outline is complete, plus anything the author needs that the earlier questions had no room for.</p><p><b>How it is used.</b> Nothing is built until you confirm. An author then maps each answer onto the matching step and section record, adds the questions, answer lists and conditional rules, and uploads the finished <code>.elicit</code> file through <em>Apply Survey Definition</em> in the Admin app.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> The notes box is the right place for things this interview cannot express: which steps should repeat per family member, wording that must change depending on who is answering, or sections that only appear for some respondents. The author reads it before touching anything else.</p>|outro||false|||||||||0|||elicit-designer|Initial publication
